7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,3],[3,3,2,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,3],[3,3,2,2,2,2,3,3,1,1,1,1,3,3,0,0,0,0,3,3,3,3],[3,3,2,2,2,2,3,3,1,1,1,1,3,3,0,0,0,0,3,3,3,3],[3,3,2,2,2,2,3,3,1,1,1,1,3,3,0,0,0,0,3,3,3,3],[3,3,2,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,3],[3,3,2,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,3],[3,3,2,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,3],[3,3,2,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,9,3],[3,3,2,2,2,2,3,3,1,1,1,1,3,3,0,0,0,0,3,3,3,3],[3,3,2,2,2,2,3,3,1,1,1,1,3,3,0,0,0,0,3,3,3,3],[3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3]]}]},"teaching_variant":"canonical","teacher_models":["claude-opus-5-5"],"explanation":"The grids are built from overlapping solid rectangles, each one a single colour, laid on a background. Small plus-shaped blots of colour 8 (5 cells each) have been stamped over the picture. The output removes every plus and shows what was underneath it.\n\n**What I checked against the train pairs.** In all three pairs, the only changed cells are 8-cells. Each one takes the colour of whichever layer would show at that spot if the plus were not there.\n- Pair 1, (4,4): this cell lies inside both the bottom 0-region and the 4-rectangle. The 4 sits on top of the 0, so the cell becomes 4.\n- Pair 1, (4,10): no rectangle's extent covers this cell, so it becomes the background colour 9.\n- Pair 3: the plus centred at (4,5) restores to the 2-column, and its arm at (4,4) falls outside every rectangle, so it becomes background 4.\n\n**Why a simple neighbour vote is not enough.** At (4,10) in pair 1, the neighbours are 0, 9, 7 and 1, and none of them is clearly right. The rule has to reason about rectangle extents and their stacking order.\n\n**The construction.**\n1. **Find the plus colour.** It is the colour whose 4-connected components are all 5-cell plus shapes. Here that is 8.\n2. **Find each colour's rectangle.** For every other colour except the background, take the bounding box of all its cells, joined across the separate visible pieces. For example, the 1-strip in test 2 is cut into three pieces by the 7 and 9 bands, and joining them recovers its full rectangle.\n3. **Work out the stacking order.** Colour A lies above colour B if some A-cell sits inside B's rectangle.\n4. **Repair each plus cell.** The candidates are the colours whose rectangle contains the cell.\n   - No candidates: the cell becomes background.\n   - Otherwise it takes the topmost candidate, meaning the one lying above the most other candidates.\n\n**Applying it to the tests.** In test 1, the plus at (0..2, 15..17) straddles the outer 1-background, the 2-frame and the 3-column. Each of its cells gets whichever of those covers it. In test 2, the pluses sitting on the 7 and 9 bands restore to 7 or 9, because those bands lie above the 2, 1 and 0 strips. Plus cells that fall outside every rectangle become background 3.\n\n**Teaching stages.** The program emits one checkpoint after each plus is repaired, then the final grid.","code":"def transform(grid):\n    g = np.array(grid)\n    bg = background(g)\n    H, W = g.shape\n    # find the plus-shaped noise colour\n    noise = None\n    for c in np.unique(g):\n        if c == bg:\n            continue\n        comps = components(g == c)\n        ok = len(comps) > 0\n        for comp in comps:\n            y0, x0, y1, x1 = bbox(comp)\n            if not (len(comp) == 5 and y1 - y0 == 2 and x1 - x0 == 2 and (y0 + 1, x0 + 1) in comp):\n                ok = False\n                break\n        if ok:\n            noise = c\n            break\n    if noise is None:\n        emit(g, \"No plus-shaped noise found; grid unchanged.\")\n        return g\n    # rectangle extent of each colour\n    boxes = {}\n    for c in np.unique(g):\n        if c in (bg, noise):\n            continue\n        ys, xs = np.where(g == c)\n        boxes[int(c)] = (ys.min(), xs.min(), ys.max(), xs.max())\n\n    def inside(b, y, x):\n        return b[0] <= y <= b[2] and b[1] <= x <= b[3]\n\n    above = {}\n    for a in boxes:\n        ys, xs = np.where(g == a)\n        for b in boxes:\n            if a == b:\n                continue\n            bb = boxes[b]\n            above[(a, b)] = bool(np.any((ys >= bb[0]) & (ys <= bb[2]) & (xs >= bb[1]) & (xs <= bb[3])))\n    out = g.copy()\n    for comp in components(g == noise):\n        for (y, x) in comp:\n            cands = [c for c in boxes if inside(boxes[c], y, x)]\n            if not cands:\n                out[y, x] = bg\n            else:\n                best = max(cands, key=lambda c: sum(above.get((c, d), False) for d in cands if d != c))\n                out[y, x] = best\n        cy, cx = bbox(comp)[0] + 1, bbox(comp)[1] + 1\n        emit(out, f\"Removed the plus centred at ({cy},{cx}), restoring each cell to the topmost rectangle covering it (or background).\")\n    emit(out, \"All noise pluses removed; final picture of layered rectangles.\")\n    return out\n","candidate_tactics":{"objects":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"**Find the plus colour.** It is the colour whose 4-connected components are all 5-cell plus shapes."}],"template":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"Small plus-shaped blots of colour 8 (5 cells each) have been stamped over the picture."}],"repair":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"- Pair 3: the plus centred at (4,5) restores to the 2-column, and its arm at (4,4) falls outside every rectangle, so it becomes background 4."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"In test 2, the pluses sitting on the 7 and 9 bands restore to 7 or 9, because those bands lie above the 2, 1 and 0 strips."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"**Teaching stages.** The program emits one checkpoint after each plus is repaired, then the final grid."}],"dynamics":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"- Pair 3: the plus centred at (4,5) restores to the 2-column, and its arm at (4,4) falls outside every rectangle, so it becomes background 4."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"Plus cells that fall outside every rectangle become background 3."}],"crop":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"**Find each colour's rectangle.** For every other colour except the background, take the bounding box of all its cells, joined across the separate visible pieces."}],"assembly":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"For example, the 1-strip in test 2 is cut into three pieces by the 7 and 9 bands, and joining them recovers its full rectangle."}],"layers":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"The grids are built from overlapping solid rectangles, each one a single colour, laid on a background."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"Each one takes the colour of whichever layer would show at that spot if the plus were not there."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"The 4 sits on top of the 0, so the cell becomes 4."}]},"provenance":{"reference_assisted":false,"query_labels_used_for_rule_diagnosis":false},"artifact_sha256":"79d7ec3d021442458eecf9d54660438e24c21b4f2cb54fe8c6054bff875bca13","task_sha256":"7e5b01df58f014cf1a820a91fd71e1b7acbfe123ac080caeb720c88f06387db7","validation":"Frozen corpus recorded successful replay on teaching fixtures; synthetic generalization not established."}
